<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Local Areas</span>
<h1>French Quarter by Motorbike: Motorbike Rental Planning in Hanoi</h1>
<p>The French Quarter is Hanoi&rsquo;s formal layer, the grid of tree-lined boulevards and colonial-era facades that begins at the southern and southeastern side of Hoan Kiem Lake and runs away from the water in long, quiet blocks, and for a rental rider it offers a particular balance, the Old Quarter&rsquo;s whole shopping-and-eating map at the doorstep without the Old Quarter&rsquo;s nightly churn inside the door. The quarter&rsquo;s character is institutional and calm, embassies, offices, hotels and apartments sharing wide pavements under old trees, and the riding it invites is the short, deliberate kind, a lake loop before breakfast, a run to the counter across the river when the week needs it, an evening in the lanes with the lights coming on. Planning a stay here is mostly planning what not to ride, because almost everything is close.</p>
<p>The rental counter is at Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, across the Red River, and every plan on this page starts and ends with that fact. Begin at the <a href="/motorbike-rental-hanoi-old-quarter/">Old Quarter motorbike rental</a> hub, see the machines and the verified prices at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> guide, browse the wider <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a>, read the <a href="/motorbike-rental-hanoi-old-quarter/hoan-kiem-rental-planning/">Hoan Kiem rental planning</a> page for the district-level picture, and see the <a href="/motorbike-rental-hanoi-old-quarter/hai-ba-trung-rental-planning/">Hai Ba Trung rental planning</a> page for the neighbouring district to the south.</p>
<nav class="toc">
<ol>
